course: money
lesson: 35
title: Unemployment: when jobs are lost
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Unemployment means not having a paid job when you want one; it can happen to anyone, often through no fault of their own, when a factory closes, a shop shuts or times get hard.
explain: Picture the news saying a big factory is closing and 500 people will lose their jobs: if each earned £500 a week, the town loses 500 × £500 = £250,000 of spending every week, so local shops and cafés suffer too.
explain: The economy goes up and down like a slow wave: in a boom jobs are easy to find, and in a recession the country makes and spends less for a while and jobs get scarcer.
explain: New machines can replace some jobs, like one robot doing the work of 10 packers, but they also create new ones, like fixing robots; people argue about whether it balances out.
explain: Looking for work can be long and upsetting, and family and friends really help; how much the government should help people out of work is something grown-ups disagree about, though both sides want people to be okay.
fact: Unemployment is not having a paid job when you want one; redundancy is losing your job because it is no longer needed.
fact: When a factory closes, local shops suffer too: 500 workers on £500 a week means 500 × £500 = £250,000 less to spend each week.
fact: The unemployment rate is the percentage of people looking for work who don't have it; 4% means 4 out of every 100.
fact: Finding a job can take time: 20 job applications with 2 replies is 1 reply in 10; an interview is a meeting to see if you get the job.
fact: Automation is machines doing work people did; one robot replacing 10 packers takes jobs, but new jobs appear too, and people disagree about whether it balances out.
fact: Furlough was government help in 2020, during the pandemic, paying part of the wages of people who couldn't work: £8 of every £10, so £1,000 of pay became £800.
fact: People argue about whether the furlough scheme worked well; it was reported to have kept many jobs, and it cost a lot of money.
fact: The economic cycle is the ups and downs of the economy: a boom is good times and a recession is bad times; unemployment falling from 6 in 100 to 4 in 100 is a sign of better times.
fact: A recession is when a country's economy shrinks for a while; GDP is the total value of everything a country makes, and shrinking 1% turns a £100 economy into £99.
fact: Jobs are not spread evenly: one town might have 8 in 100 unemployed and another 3 in 100, a difference of 5 in 100.
fact: Welfare is government help for people who need it, such as £400 a month compared with £1,600 from a low-paid job.
fact: Some say people out of work should get more help; others say too much help means less need to find work; both want people to be okay but disagree on how.
fact: Losing a job is not a sign that someone is lazy or bad; most job losses come from changes the worker cannot control.
exercise: What is unemployment? | Not having a paid job when you want one | Choosing not to work because you are retired | Having two jobs at the same time | Working without being paid as a volunteer
exercise: A factory closes and 500 people each lose £500 a week of pay. How much less money is that in the town each week? | £250,000 | £1,000 | £25,000 | £2,500,000
exercise: Why do local shops suffer when a factory closes? | People who lost their pay spend less in them | The shops have to pay the factory's bills | Shops are not allowed to open without a factory | The factory workers used to run the shops
exercise: The news says unemployment is 4%. What does it mean? | 4 out of every 100 people looking for work can't find it | 4 people in the whole country have no job | 4 out of every 10 people have no job | Everybody works 4 hours less each week
exercise: Uncle Dave applied for 20 jobs and heard back from 2. What is that as a fraction? | 1 in 10 | 1 in 2 | 2 in 10 | 1 in 20
exercise: A robot now does the work of 10 packers. What is this called? | Automation | Redundancy pay | Inflation | Furlough
exercise: Besides taking some jobs, what else does automation do? | It creates new jobs, like fixing robots | It makes every job in the country disappear | It pays the old workers twice as much | It has no effect on any jobs at all
exercise: On furlough Mia's mum got £8 of every £10 of her £1,000 pay. How much did she get? | £800 | £200 | £80 | £1,080
exercise: What was furlough? | Government help paying part of wages when people couldn't work | A holiday that every worker in the country had to take in the summer | A fine paid by people who stayed at home instead of going to work | A new kind of job that was created for the first time in 2020
exercise: Last year 6 in 100 couldn't find work; this year it's 4 in 100. What does that show? | Jobs are easier to find than last year | Jobs are harder to find than last year | Nothing has changed at all | Everyone has found a job now
exercise: The news says "We are in a recession." What does it mean? | The country makes and spends less for a while | Everyone in the country has lost their job at the same time | Prices have gone down by half overnight in every shop | The government has closed all the shops for a whole year
exercise: An economy worth £100 shrinks by 1%. What is it worth now? | £99 | £90 | £1 | £101
exercise: What is GDP? | The total value of everything a country makes | The number of people in a country who have no job | The government's bank account | The price of a loaf of bread
exercise: Town A has 8 in 100 unemployed and Town B has 3 in 100. What is the difference? | 5 in 100 | 11 in 100 | 3 in 100 | 8 in 100
exercise: Two people argue: "More help for people out of work!" and "Too much help means less need to work!" What is true? | Both want people to be okay but disagree on how | One of them secretly wants people to suffer and be poor | Neither of them has any reason at all for what they say | They are really arguing about football, not about jobs
test: Your mate says, "People who lose their jobs are just lazy, innit." What's the fairer answer? | Most job losses come from changes they can't control, like a factory closing | He's right, only lazy people ever lose jobs | Losing a job means you did something illegal | Jobs are only lost by people who are rich
test: Kai's friend's dad works at the closing factory. Kai's mate says, "Not our problem, we don't work there." What would really happen? | Less spending in town could hurt shops and other jobs too | Nothing would change for anyone else in town | The town would get richer without the factory | All the shops in town would get the factory's money instead
test: Uncle Dave sighs, "Applied to 20, heard back from 2 — no, 3 actually." How many didn't reply? | 17 | 18 | 23 | 3
test: A worker on TV says, "Machines nicked our jobs." ("Nicked" means stole.) A robot engineer says, "Machines made my job." What's the fair view? | Both are true for them; people argue about whether it balances out | The worker is lying, robots never replace anyone | The engineer is lying, robots never create jobs | Neither, robots don't exist in warehouses
test: Ben's cousin from Leeds says, "Jobs, it depends where you live, innit, our town's dead quiet." What does this mean? | Some areas have lots of work and others very little | Every town has exactly the same number of jobs as the next one | Jobs are moved around between towns each week by law | Quiet towns are not allowed by the law to have any jobs at all
test: What would happen to unemployment if the economy went into a long recession? | It would probably go up, as firms sell less and cut jobs | It would fall to zero, as everyone rushes to find work to do | Nothing, recessions don't affect jobs at all | Everyone would get a pay rise to cheer them up
test: Ruby asks, "Recession, is that bad?" Her mate goes, "It's when they recess at school, like break time, lol." What's the real meaning? | The country's economy shrinks for a while | A long playtime given to all schools | A holiday for everyone who works | The time when shops sell everything cheap
test: Mia's mum, from Cork, says, "We were on furlough, sure we'd have been grand without it, like." ("Grand" means fine.) Her neighbour says it saved his job. What's true? | People's experiences differ, and people argue about how well it worked | Everyone agrees it was a total waste | Everyone agrees it was perfect and cheap | Furlough never happened anywhere
test: In a cartoon, the town's only factory closes and the next day everyone has a new job. What's unrealistic? | Finding work can take a long time and is often hard | Nothing, new jobs always appear by the very next day | Factories never close down in real life, only in stories | People who live in towns never really need jobs to live
test: Grace hears two people argue about help for people out of work. The help is £400 a month and a low-paid job pays £1,600. How much more does the job pay? | £1,200 | £2,000 | £400 | £4
test: The economy is £100 and grows 2% — no wait, 3%. What is it worth now? | £103 | £102 | £97 | £130
test: Leo hears his dad, a Scot, say, "Jobs are easy tae find the noo." ("The noo" means right now.) Last year it was harder. What has likely happened? | The economy has picked up, and unemployment has fallen | A recession has started and jobs are vanishing | Everybody in Scotland has retired | The government has banned job interviews
exam: It is given that five hundred employees, each remunerated at £500 per week, lose their employment. Determine the aggregate weekly reduction in local earnings. | £250,000 | £1,000 | £25,000 | £2,500,000
exam: Ascertain the value of an economy formerly valued at £100 subsequent to a contraction of one per cent. | £99 | £90 | £1 | £101
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the unemployment rate? | The percentage of persons seeking employment who are without it | The total number of persons resident in a country | The percentage of employees in receipt of a pay rise | The number of factories closed in a given year
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the debate upon assistance to persons without employment. | Both parties wish such persons to be well, but differ as to the means | One party wishes such persons to suffer hardship as a form of punishment | Neither party advances any reason for its position | The matter is not the subject of any disagreement
